#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186075049 :tutorial/content "As we've seen, the solutions of a linear system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}a_{1}x + b_{1}y &=c_{1} \\\\ a_{2}x + b_{2}y &=c_{2}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\ncorrespond to the points of intersection of the two lines. There are three possibilities:\n\n- Case $(a)$ shows two parallel lines.\n\n  ![](images/a1/a1e58afecd7b919f6fd77b48f7bcb7219e54d2f86f8ef07df619505a4643bdd4.png)\n\n  Because there are no points of intersection, the system has *no solutions*. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope but *different* $y$-intercepts.\n\n- Case $(b)$ shows two lines intersecting at a single point.\n\n  ![](images/a4/a466524eb95d85e6c383a37955fffbf02fc9391b0b09a85fce7f44ab934bdc91.png)\n\n  Because there is a single point of intersection, the system has *one solution*.\n\n- Case $(c)$ shows two lines that coincide.\n\n  ![](images/c7/c73a9f1bfbdd4efa1826b77a7bce1a9e1cbb13e61d718ffe18404e13a31a34f4.png)\n\n  Because there are infinitely many points of intersection, the system has *infinitely many* solutions. This happens whenever the lines have the *same* slope and *same* $y$-intercept. In such cases, the two lines are nonzero multiples of each other." :tutorial/math-academy-id 8708 :tutorial/title "The Intersection of Two Lines"}]}
